.Fo 
.Fa *custom_ext_free_cb
.Fc
(SSL *s, unsigned int ext_type,
.Bd -literal
                                    const unsigned char *out,
                                    void *add_arg);
.Ed
.Ft typedef int
.Fo 
.Fa *custom_ext_parse_cb
.Fc
(SSL *s, unsigned int ext_type,
.Bd -literal
                                    const unsigned char *in,
                                    size_t inlen, int *al,
                                    void *parse_arg);
.Ed
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_CTX_add_client_custom_ext
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned int ext_type"
.Fa "custom_ext_add_cb add_cb"
.Fa "custom_ext_free_cb free_cb"
.Fa "void *add_arg"
.Fa "custom_ext_parse_cb parse_cb"
.Fa "void *parse_arg"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_CTX_add_server_custom_ext
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned int ext_type"
.Fa "custom_ext_add_cb add_cb"
.Fa "custom_ext_free_cb free_cb"
.Fa "void *add_arg"
.Fa "custom_ext_parse_cb parse_cb"
.Fa "void *parse_arg"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_extension_supported
.Fa "unsigned int ext_type"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_custom_ext
adds a custom extension for a TLS/DTLS client or server for all
supported protocol versions with extension type
.Fa ext_type
and callbacks
.Fa add_cb ,
.Fa free_cb
and
.Fa parse_cb
(see the
.Sx EXTENSION CALLBACKS
section below).
The
.Fa context
value determines which messages and under what conditions the extension
will be added/parsed (see the
.Sx EXTENSION CONTEXTS
section below).
.Pp
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_client_custom_ext
adds a custom extension for a TLS/DTLS client with extension type
.Fa ext_type
and callbacks
.Fa add_cb ,
.Fa free_cb
and
.Fa parse_cb .
This function is similar to
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_custom_ext
except it only applies to clients, uses the older style of callbacks,
and implicitly sets the
.Fa context
value to:
.Bd -literal
 SSL_EXT_TLS1_2_AND_BELOW_ONLY | SSL_EXT_CLIENT_HELLO
 | SSL_EXT_TLS1_2_SERVER_HELLO | SSL_EXT_IGNORE_ON_RESUMPTION
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_server_custom_ext
adds a custom extension for a TLS/DTLS server with extension type
.Fa ext_type
and callbacks
.Fa add_cb ,
.Fa free_cb
and
.Fa parse_cb .
This function is similar to
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_custom_ext
except it only applies to servers, uses the older style of callbacks,
and implicitly sets the
.Fa context
value to the same as for
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_client_custom_ext
above.
.Pp
The
.Fa ext_type
parameter corresponds to the
.Sy extension_type
field of RFC5246 et al.
It is
.Sy not
a NID. In all cases the extension type must not be handled by OpenSSL
internally or an error occurs.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_extension_supported
returns 1 if the extension
.Fa ext_type
is handled internally by OpenSSL and 0 otherwise.
.Sh EXTENSION CALLBACKS
The callback
.Fa add_cb
is called to send custom extension data to be included in various TLS
messages.
The
.Fa ext_type
parameter is set to the extension type which will be added and
.Fa add_arg
to the value set when the extension handler was added.
When using the new style callbacks the
.Fa context
parameter will indicate which message is currently being constructed
e.g.
for the ClientHello it will be set to
.Sy SSL_EXT_CLIENT_HELLO .
.Pp
If the application wishes to include the extension
.Fa ext_type
it should set
.Sy *out
to the extension data, set
.Sy *outlen
to the length of the extension data and return 1.
.Pp
If the
.Fa add_cb
does not wish to include the extension it must return 0.
.Pp
If
.Fa add_cb
returns -1 a fatal handshake error occurs using the TLS alert value
specified in
.Sy *al .
.Pp
When constructing the ClientHello, if
.Fa add_cb
is set to NULL a zero length extension is added for
.Fa ext_type .
For all other messages if
.Fa add_cb
is set to NULL then no extension is added.
.Pp
When constructing a Certificate message the callback will be called for
each certificate in the message.
The
.Sy x
parameter will indicate the current certificate and the
.Sy chainidx
parameter will indicate the position of the certificate in the message.
The first certificate is always the end entity certificate and has a
.Sy chainidx
value of 0. The certificates are in the order that they were received in
the Certificate message.
.Pp
For all messages except the ServerHello and EncryptedExtensions every
registered
.Fa add_cb
is always called to see if the application wishes to add an extension
(as long as all requirements of the specified
.Fa context
are met).
.Pp
For the ServerHello and EncryptedExtension messages every registered
.Fa add_cb
is called once if and only if the requirements of the specified
.Fa context
are met and the corresponding extension was received in the ClientHello.
That is, if no corresponding extension was received in the ClientHello
then
.Fa add_cb
will not be called.
.Pp
If an extension is added (that is
.Fa add_cb
returns 1)
.Fa free_cb
is called (if it is set) with the value of
.Sy out
set by the add callback.
It can be used to free up any dynamic extension data set by
.Fa add_cb .
Since
.Sy out
is constant (to permit use of constant data in
.Fa add_cb )
applications may need to cast away const to free the data.
.Pp
The callback
.Fa parse_cb
receives data for TLS extensions.
The callback is only called if the extension is present and relevant for
the context (see
.Sx EXTENSION CONTEXTS
below).
.Pp
The extension data consists of
.Sy inlen
bytes in the buffer
.Sy in
for the extension
.Fa ext_type .
.Pp
If the message being parsed is a TLSv1.3 compatible Certificate message
then
.Fa parse_cb
will be called for each certificate contained within the message.
The
.Sy x
parameter will indicate the current certificate and the
.Sy chainidx
parameter will indicate the position of the certificate in the message.
The first certificate is always the end entity certificate and has a
.Sy chainidx
value of 0.
.Pp
If the
.Fa parse_cb
considers the extension data acceptable it must return 1. If it returns
0 or a negative value a fatal handshake error occurs using the TLS alert
value specified in
.Sy *al .
.Pp
The buffer
.Sy in
is a temporary internal buffer which will not be valid after the
callback returns.
.Sh EXTENSION CONTEXTS
An extension context defines which messages and under which conditions
an extension should be added or expected.
The context is built up by performing a bitwise OR of multiple
pre-defined values together.
The valid context values are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It SSL_EXT_TLS_ONLY
The extension is only allowed in TLS
.It SSL_EXT_DTLS_ONLY
The extension is only allowed in DTLS
.It SSL_EXT_TLS_IMPLEMENTATION_ONLY
The extension is allowed in DTLS, but there is only a TLS implementation
available (so it is ignored in DTLS).
.It SSL_EXT_SSL3_ALLOWED
Extensions are not typically defined for SSLv3. Setting this value will
allow the extension in SSLv3. Applications will not typically need to
use this.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_2_AND_BELOW_ONLY
The extension is only defined for TLSv1.2/DTLSv1.2 and below.
Servers will ignore this extension if it is present in the ClientHello
and TLSv1.3 is negotiated.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_ONLY
The extension is only defined for TLS1.3 and above.
Servers will ignore this extension if it is present in the ClientHello
and TLSv1.2 or below is negotiated.
.It SSL_EXT_IGNORE_ON_RESUMPTION
The extension will be ignored during parsing if a previous session is
being successfully resumed.
.It SSL_EXT_CLIENT_HELLO
The extension may be present in the ClientHello message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_2_SERVER_HELLO
The extension may be present in a TLSv1.2 or below compatible
ServerHello message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_SERVER_HELLO
The extension may be present in a TLSv1.3 compatible ServerHello
message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_ENCRYPTED_EXTENSIONS
The extension may be present in an EncryptedExtensions message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_HELLO_RETRY_REQUEST
The extension may be present in a HelloRetryRequest message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_CERTIFICATE
The extension may be present in a TLSv1.3 compatible Certificate
message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_NEW_SESSION_TICKET
The extension may be present in a TLSv1.3 compatible NewSessionTicket
message.
.It SSL_EXT_TLS1_3_CERTIFICATE_REQUEST
The extension may be present in a TLSv1.3 compatible CertificateRequest
message.
.El
.Pp
The context must include at least one message value (otherwise the
extension will never be used).
.Sh NOTES
The
.Fa add_arg
and
.Fa parse_arg
parameters can be set to arbitrary values which will be passed to the
corresponding callbacks.
They can, for example, be used to store the extension data received in a
convenient structure or pass the extension data to be added or freed
when adding extensions.
.Pp
If the same custom extension type is received multiple times a fatal
.Sy decode_error
alert is sent and the handshake aborts.
If a custom extension is received in a ServerHello/EncryptedExtensions
message which was not sent in the ClientHello a fatal
.Sy unsupported_extension
alert is sent and the handshake is aborted.
The ServerHello/EncryptedExtensions
.Fa add_cb
callback is only called if the corresponding extension was received in
the ClientHello.
This is compliant with the TLS specifications.
This behaviour ensures that each callback is called at most once and
that an application can never send unsolicited extensions.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_custom_ext ,
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_client_custom_ext
and
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_server_custom_ext
return 1 for success and 0 for failure.
A failure can occur if an attempt is made to add the same
.Fa ext_type
more than once, if an attempt is made to use an extension type handled
internally by OpenSSL or if an internal error occurs (for example a
memory allocation failure).
.Pp
.Fn SSL_extension_supported
returns 1 if the extension
.Fa ext_type
is handled internally by OpenSSL and 0 otherwise.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn SSL_CTX_add_custom_ext
function was added in OpenSSL 1.1.1.
